   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to immigration status of\nregistered students",
    "basePrintNo": "S3475",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S3475-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 55,
       "fullName": "Samra Brouk",
       "imgName": "1317_Samra_Brouk.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1500,
       "sessionMemberId": 2050,
       "sessionYear": 2023,
       "shortName": "BROUK"
      },
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 31,
       "fullName": "Robert Jackson",
       "imgName": "1050_Robert_Jackson.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1230,
       "sessionMemberId": 2017,
       "sessionYear": 2023,
       "shortName": "JACKSON"
      }
     ],
     "size": 2
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §§355-f & 6206-a, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S3475\n\nSPONSOR: STAVISKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to immigration status of\nregistered students\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis legislation would make it unlawful for any employee or officer of\nthe city or state university to request the specific details of a\nstudent's immigration status except when required by federal law, pursu-\nant to court order, or as required in connection with tuition or finan-\ncial aid eligibility.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Article 8 of the Education law is amended and a new section\n355-d is added which outlines circumstances under which immigration\nstatus may be requested. The Board of Trustees of the city and state\nuniversity will promulgate standards for all colleges and universities\nwithin the system to insure that a student shall not be asked any ques-\ntions on immigration status other than any information required by\nsubdivision one of this act. In no case shall anyone be required to\nprovide any information on a student's lawful immigration status, as a\nrefugee, or as an immigrant from any specific nation.\n\nSection 2. Article 125 of the Education Law is amended and a new section\n6206-a is added with the same wording as in Section 1.\n\nSection 3. Severability. If any part of this act is judged by any court\nof competent jurisdiction to be invalid after exhaustive judicial\nreview, it will not affect, impair or invalidate the remainder of the\nact but will be confined only to the portion directly involved in the\ncontroversy in which judgment shall have been rendered.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis proposal prohibits SUNY and CUNY employees from inquiring about a\nstudent's immigration status in order to prevent the federal government\nfrom deporting immigrant students. It would neutralize efforts to\nrequire universities to compile data regarding the number of foreign\nstudents, their countries of origin, and the programs in which they are\nenrolled. SUNY and CUNY would be prohibited from determining if a\nstudent was from a specific Muslim-majority nation targeted by the ban,\nas well as whether the student had proper immigration status or was\nundocumented. Additionally, SUNY and CUNY would be prohibited from\nassisting the federal government in enforcing federal immigration laws\nunless such participation was expressly required as a lawful and related\ncondition of receiving federal higher education funds.\n\nNew York has always been a progressive leader in human rights. As a\nnation of immigrants, our diversity is what has made us a world leader\nand New York must reaffirm its commitment to all people, regard less of\nrace, religion or national origin. Students, wherever they come from and\nregardless of their immigration status, must be free to study unfettered\nby the fear that they might end up on a deportation list developed by\nthe very university at which they are studying.  SUNY has an estimated\n22,140 international students with 320 from the seven countries on the\ntravel ban list. CUNY has 8,881 foreign students with 120 who would be\naffected by the travel ban. Our institutions of higher learning should\nfocus on educating future generations of New Yorkers who will improve\nour state and grow our economy - not prosecuting students based on their\nreligion or nationality. SUNY and CUNY schools must remain places of\ndignified higher learning, not mechanisms to spy on students.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017-2018: A.6558/S.4079 - Referred to Higher Education.\n\n2019-2020: A.5273-A/S.2550-A - Amend and recommit to Higher Education.\n\n2021-2022: A.No same as/S.2778 - Sent to Assembly.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
